---

<ArticleLayout
    title={title}
    description={description}
    pubDate={pubDate}
    updatedDate={updatedDate}
    authorSlug={authorSlug}
    category={category}
    categorySlug={categorySlug}
    readingTime={readingTime}
    image={image}
    imageAlt={imageAlt}
    headings={headings}
>
  <p class="lead">
    Spent three months with constant neck pain before figuring out my monitor was too low. Fixed it in five minutes.
    Wish someone had just shown me how to set this up properly from day one.
  </p>

  <h2 id="why-ergonomics">Why Ergonomics Matter for Remote Workers</h2>

  <p>
    Working remote means sitting at your desk way more than you'd think. No walking to meetings. No commute. Just eight
    to twelve hours hunched over a screen. Get your setup wrong and you'll feel it - back pain, stiff neck, sore wrists.
  </p>

  <h2 id="chair-setup">Start With Your Chair</h2>

  <p>
    Everything else builds from this. Mess up the <a href="/gear/best-ergonomic-chairs">chair</a> and nothing else
    works.
  </p>

  <h3>Seat Height</h3>
  <p>
    Adjust the seat until your feet land flat on the floor. Thighs roughly parallel to the ground - angled down a bit is
    fine too. Knees bent around ninety degrees. Leave a small gap between the seat edge and the backs of your knees,
    couple finger widths at least.
  </p>

  <h3>Lumbar Support</h3>
  <p>
    That lumbar bump? Position it at the small of your back - the curved part of your lower spine. Should feel
    supportive without pushing you forward. No adjustable lumbar on your chair? A rolled up towel works in a pinch. Or
    grab a small pillow.
  </p>

  <h3>Backrest Recline</h3>
  <p>
    Fun fact: <a href="https://www.cdc.gov/niosh/topics/ergonomics/default.html" target="_blank" rel="nofollow">sitting
    perfectly straight at ninety degrees puts more pressure on your spine than leaning back</a> according to ergonomics
    research from NIOSH at the CDC. Weird,
    right? Recline slightly - somewhere between one hundred and one hundred ten degrees. Let the chair do the work.
  </p>

  <h3>Armrests</h3>
  <p>
    Armrests should let your elbows rest at ninety degrees with shoulders relaxed. Not shrugged up toward your ears. If
    armrests bump your desk when you roll close, lower them or remove them entirely.
  </p>

  <h2 id="monitor-position">Monitor Height and Distance</h2>

  <p>
    This is where most neck pain comes from.
  </p>

  <h3>Height</h3>
  <p>
    Top of your screen at eye level or slightly below. Looking slightly downward at the center - about fifteen to twenty
    degrees. <a href="https://www.aoa.org/healthy-eyes/caring-for-your-eyes/computer-usage" target="_blank"
                rel="nofollow">Keep it roughly arms length away, twenty to twenty-six inches or so</a> per American
    Optometric Association recommendations.
  </p>

  <h3>Distance</h3>
  <p>
    Squinting to read? Bump up the font size. Moving the screen closer just trades eye strain for neck strain.
  </p>

  <h3>Angle</h3>
  <p>
    Tilt the screen back a bit (ten to twenty degrees) to cut glare. Windows work best to the side of your desk rather
    than directly behind or in front of you.
  </p>

  <h3>Dual Monitors</h3>
  <p>
    Running dual monitors? If you use both equally, put the center gap right in front of you. Primary monitor only?
    Center that one and angle the secondary toward you.
  </p>

  <h3>Laptop Users</h3>
  <p>
    <a href="/gear/best-laptops-remote-work">Laptop</a> users have it worst. The keyboard and screen are stuck together
    so you can't position them independently.
    Best fix: external <a href="/gear/best-monitors-remote-work">monitor</a> plus external keyboard and mouse. Next
    best: laptop stand plus external keyboard. At
    minimum, raise the screen even if keyboard position suffers.
  </p>

  <h2 id="keyboard-mouse">Keyboard and Mouse</h2>

  <h3>Keyboard Position</h3>
  <p>
    Put your keyboard directly in front of you. Centered with your body. Elbows close, bent somewhere between ninety and
    one hundred ten degrees. Wrists straight - not bent up or down.
  </p>

  <p>
    Here's a big one: those little legs on the back of your keyboard that tilt it up? They actually increase wrist
    strain. Keep the keyboard flat or even tilted slightly the other way (front edge higher).
  </p>

  <h3>Mouse Position</h3>
  <p>
    Mouse goes right next to the keyboard at the same level. Reaching to the side or forward for your mouse causes
    shoulder problems. Got a full keyboard with a number pad? Think about switching to a compact one. Brings the mouse
    closer in.
  </p>

  <h3>Wrist Position</h3>
  <p>
    Wrists should hover while you type. Don't let them rest on the desk. Those wrist rest pads? They're for breaks
    between typing sessions, not for resting on during typing.
  </p>

  <h2 id="desk-height">Desk Height Check</h2>

  <p>
    Standard desks are twenty-nine to thirty inches. Works for people around five ten to six feet tall. Everyone else
    needs to adjust.
  </p>

  <p>
    Once your chair is right, check this: can you rest your hands on the keyboard with elbows at ninety degrees? Too
    high? Raise your chair and add a footrest. Too low? Desk risers or a keyboard tray.
  </p>

  <h2 id="lighting">Lighting Matters</h2>

  <p>
    Bad lighting equals eye strain and headaches. Pretty simple equation.
  </p>

  <h3>Screen Brightness</h3>
  <p>
    Screen brightness should roughly match the room around it. Monitor looks like it's glowing? Too bright. Looks gray
    and washed out? Too dim.
  </p>

  <h3>Room Lighting</h3>
  <p>
    Keep overhead lights from shining directly onto your screen. Position the screen at right angles to windows. If it
    faces a window directly, you'll fight glare all day.
  </p>

  <p>
    Some people swear by bias lighting - a soft light strip behind the monitor. Reduces the harsh contrast between
    bright screen and dark room. Blue light filtering helps at night. Built-in features like Night Shift work fine.
  </p>

  <h2 id="standing-ergonomics">Standing Desk Tips</h2>

  <p>
    If you've got a <a href="/gear/best-standing-desks">standing desk</a>, you still need proper positioning for both
    sitting and standing.
  </p>

  <h3>Standing Position</h3>
  <p>
    Standing: elbows still at ninety degrees. Monitor at eye level (higher than sitting position). Weight even on both
    feet. An anti-fatigue mat makes a huge difference - standing on hard floors gets painful.
  </p>

  <h3>Sit-Stand Ratio</h3>
  <p>
    Don't try to stand all day starting out. Begin with fifteen to thirty minute intervals. Build up over weeks. A good
    ratio: forty-five minutes sitting, fifteen minutes standing per hour. But honestly, just listen to how your body
    feels.
  </p>

  <h2 id="common-mistakes">Mistakes That Mess Everyone Up</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Monitor too low.</strong> Especially laptop users. Your neck should be neutral, not craned forward.</li>
    <li><strong>Keyboard tilted up.</strong> Those rear legs seem helpful. They're not.</li>
    <li><strong>Mouse too far away.</strong> Reaching sideways strains your shoulder.</li>
    <li><strong>No lumbar support.</strong> Your lower back has a natural curve. Support it.</li>
    <li><strong>Sitting still for hours.</strong> Even perfect posture becomes painful without movement.</li>
    <li><strong>Ignoring early warning signs.</strong> Mild discomfort becomes real pain if you ignore it.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="accessories">Ergonomic Accessories</h2>

  <ComparisonTable columns={accessoriesColumns} rows={accessoriesRows}/>

  <h3>Most Impactful Accessories</h3>

  <p>
    <strong>Monitor arm:</strong> Essential if your monitor cannot reach proper height on its stand. Also frees desk
    space.
  </p>

  <div class="my-6">
    <AffiliateButton href="/go/monitor-arm" text="Check Monitor Arm Prices"/>
  </div>

  <p>
    <strong>Laptop stand:</strong> Mandatory for laptop users. Raises screen to eye level.
  </p>

  <div class="my-6">
    <AffiliateButton href="/go/laptop-stand" text="Check Laptop Stand Prices"/>
  </div>

  <p>
    <strong>External <a href="/gear/best-mechanical-keyboards">keyboard</a> and mouse:</strong> Required with a laptop
    stand. Allows independent positioning of screen
    and input devices.
  </p>

  <h2 id="micro-breaks">Take Breaks</h2>

  <p>
    No setup replaces movement. Get these into your routine:
  </p>

  <h3>Every 20-30 Minutes</h3>
  <p>
    <a href="https://www.osha.gov/etools/computer-workstations/components/workstation-environment" target="_blank"
       rel="nofollow">Look at something across the room or out the window for twenty seconds</a> as recommended by OSHA
    guidelines. Consciously blink. Roll your shoulders
    around.
  </p>

  <h3>Every 60-90 Minutes</h3>
  <p>
    Stand up. Take a lap around your place. Five or ten minutes. Grab water. Stretch your neck and wrists a bit.
  </p>

  <h3>Every 2-3 Hours</h3>
  <p>
    Take a longer break. Go outside if you can. Do some actual stretching.
  </p>

  <h2 id="checklist">Quick Check</h2>

  <p>
    Run through this list:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Feet flat on floor or footrest</li>
    <li>Thighs parallel to floor</li>
    <li>Lumbar support against your lower back</li>
    <li>Back against the backrest</li>
    <li>Armrests at elbow height</li>
    <li>Shoulders down and relaxed</li>
    <li>Elbows at ninety to one hundred ten degrees</li>
    <li>Wrists straight</li>
    <li>Keyboard flat</li>
    <li>Mouse next to keyboard at same level</li>
    <li>Monitor top at or below eye level</li>
    <li>Screen roughly arms length from face</li>
    <li>Brightness matches room</li>
    <li>No glare on screen</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

  <FAQSection faqs={faqItems}/>

  <h2>The Real Secret</h2>

  <p>
    Perfect setup is personal. These guidelines get you close but you need to adjust based on how your body feels.
    Something uncomfortable? Try small changes.
  </p>

  <p>
    And remember - the best position is the next one. Move throughout the day. Sit. Stand. Walk. No amount of ergonomic
    gear beats simply not staying frozen in one spot for hours.
  </p>

  <Fragment slot="full-width">
    <RelatedArticles articles={relatedArticles}/>
  </Fragment>
</ArticleLayout>
